To implement the `Cache<K, V>` interface and the `MapCache<K, V>` class in TypeScript, we need to ensure that the class correctly implements the methods defined in the interface. The `Cache<K, V>` interface will have `get` and `set` methods, which should allow us to retrieve and store values associated with keys of type `K`. The `MapCache<K, V>` class will use a JavaScript `Map` to store these key-value pairs. We must also ensure that our implementation typechecks under the `--strict` flag, which enforces strict type checking rules, including no implicit any types and ensuring all properties are initialized.

Edge cases to consider include handling of undefined or null values when setting or getting items, ensuring that the keys are unique, and managing the size of the cache if necessary (e.g., implementing a least recently used eviction policy).

ANSWER:

```typescript
interface Cache<K, V> {
    get(key: K): V | undefined;
    set(key: K, value: V): void;
}

class MapCache<K, V> implements Cache<K, V> {
    private map: Map<K, V>;

    constructor() {
        this.map = new Map<K, V>();
    }

    get(key: K): V | undefined {
        return this.map.get(key);
    }

