```

rn-045.text:
To compose two styles in a React Native component, you can pass an array of style objects to the `style` prop. This method allows you to combine multiple styles, with the latter styles in the array having higher precedence over the former ones in case of conflicting properties. Edge cases include ensuring that the styles are correctly defined and that there are no typos or syntax errors that could prevent the component from rendering as expected.

ANSWER:

```jsx
import React from 'react';
import { View, Text, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';

const ComposedStylesComponent = () => {
  return (
    <View style={[styles.container, styles.additionalStyle]}>
      <Text style={[styles.text, styles.boldText]}>Hello, World!</Text>
    </View>
  );
};

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    flex: 1,
    justifyContent: 'center',
    alignItems: 'center',
    backgroundColor: '#f0f0f0',
  },
  additionalStyle: {
    padding: 20,
    borderRadius: 10,
  },
  text: {
    fontSize: 16,
    color: '#333',
  },
  boldText: {
    fontWeight: 'bold',
  },
});

export default ComposedStylesComponent;
```

rn-047.text:
To create a React Native FlatList with a keyExtractor that falls back to the index when an item has no id, I will define a keyExtractor function that checks if each item has an id property. If the id is present, it will use that as the key; otherwise, it will use the index of the item in the list. This approach ensures that each item in the list has a unique key, even if some items do not have an id. Edge cases include handling lists where all items lack an id, ensuring performance with large lists, and maintaining the order of items when keys are generated from indices.

ANSWER:

```jsx
<FlatList
  data={items}
  keyExtractor={(item, index) => item.id ? item.id.toString() : index.toString()}
  renderItem={({ item }) => (
    <Text>{item.name}</Text>
  )}
/>
```

rn-048.text:
To keep a mutable value across renders in React Native without causing a re-render, I will use the `useRef` hook. This hook allows me to create a reference to a value that persists between renders, which is useful for storing values that do not need to trigger a re-render when they change. Edge cases include ensuring that the mutable value does not affect the component's state or props, as this could lead to unexpected behavior or re-renders. Additionally, I must be cautious about updating the mutable value in a way that could cause side effects or race conditions, especially if it is being used in asynchronous operations.

ANSWER:

```jsx
import React, { useRef } from 'react';
import { View, Text, Button } from 'react-native';

const MutableValueComponent = () => {
  const mutableValue = useRef(0);

  const incrementValue = () => {
    mutableValue.current += 1;
  };

  return (
    <View>
      <Text>Mutable Value: {mutableValue.current}</Text>
      <Button title="Increment" onPress={incrementValue} />
    </View>
  );
};

export default MutableValueComponent;
```

rn-050.text:
To create a React Native component that renders nothing when a specific prop is falsy, I will define a functional component that checks the value of this prop. If the prop is falsy (i.e., `null`, `undefined`, `false`, `0`, `NaN`, or an empty string), the component will return `null` to indicate that nothing should be rendered. This approach ensures that the component behaves correctly in all scenarios where the prop might not have a truthy value.

ANSWER:

```jsx
import React from 'react';

const ConditionalRenderer = ({ shouldRender }) => {
  if (!shouldRender) {
    return null;
  }
  // Render something here when shouldRender is truthy
  return <Text>Content to render</Text>;
};

export default ConditionalRenderer;
```
